=================================================================
=                        INTRODUCTION                           =
=================================================================

 Welcome to the zmax editor! zmax is different from editors
 you might be used to in that it is modal, meaning that it has
 different modes for editing text. The primary modes you will
 use are Normal mode and Insert mode. While in Normal mode, the
 keys you type won't actually type text. Instead, they will
 perform various actions with the text. This allows for more
 efficient editing. This tutor will teach you how you can make
 use of zmax's modal editing features. To begin, ensure your
 CapsLock key is not pressed and hold the j key until you reach
 the first lesson.

 This tutor opens in an unnamed buffer.
 * To quit without saving changes, use :q!.
 * To save your changes to a file, use :w <path> and then :q.








=================================================================
=                      0.1 KEYMAP PRESETS                       =
=================================================================
 zmax ships eight keymap presets. The default is spacemacs:
 vim keys, plus the SPC (space) leader and the Emacs C-x
 prefix. Holding either one pops up a which-key menu of the
 keys that can follow it.

 The presets are spacemacs (the default), vim, helix, kakoune,
 emacs, cua, micro and nano. Switch at any time with

     :keymap helix

 or permanently with keymap = "..." in config.toml, which
 :config-open opens for you.

 Chapters 1 to 13 teach the selection-first keys of the helix
 preset, so run :keymap helix before working through them, and
 :keymap spacemacs to return to the default. Chapters 14 and
 later are written for the default spacemacs keymap; where the
 helix preset differs, the difference is spelled out.

=================================================================
=                  0.2 FINDING YOUR WAY AROUND                  =
=================================================================
 Nothing in this tutorial has to be memorised. These find
 everything else:

 SPC SPC    the command palette: fuzzy-search every command
 SPC ?      the same palette (also bound in the helix preset)
 SPC h h    the Help browser: commands, keys and topics
 :          the command line; Tab completes command names

 A half-remembered chord can always be finished by reading the
 which-key popup that appears while you hold the prefix.

 :tutor (or SPC h t) reopens this tutorial from any buffer.

