 * Enter grows the selection to the next enclosing syntax
   node; Backspace shrinks it.
 * diw, ci", ya(, vip work on textual objects; dif and vaC
   work on tree-sitter objects (f function, C class,
   a parameter, c comment, T test).
 * ]m and [m jump between functions, ]/ and [/ between
   comments, ]] and [[ between sections.
 * SPC v expands the region; the helix preset uses A-o, A-i
   and the sibling keys A-n and A-p.










=================================================================
=            18.1 FILTERING THROUGH A SHELL COMMAND             =
=================================================================
 A selection can be handed to an external command and replaced
 by whatever that command prints.

 In the default keymap ! is the vim filter operator: !! filters
 the current line, !j the next two lines, !G to the end of the
 file. zmax then asks for the command.

 Typed forms work on the current selection in any keymap:

   :pipe sort           replace the selection with its output
   :pipe-to wc -l       run it, keep the selection unchanged
   :insert-output date  insert the output before the selection
   :append-output date  insert it after
   :sh make -j8         run a command, show its output

 The helix preset puts these on | , $ , ! and A-! .



=================================================================
=                 18.2 THE INTEGRATED TERMINAL                  =
=================================================================
 :terminal opens a real PTY running your $SHELL in a pane, so
 curses programs, colours and job control all work.

 SPC p ' opens the same terminal in the project root.

 Inside the terminal every key belongs to the shell, so the
 window commands need their own leader:

   C-\ then s     split the terminal horizontally
   C-\ then v     split it vertically
   C-\ then h j k l    move focus between panes
   C-\ then q     close the pane
   F12            detach and leave the shell running

 Clicking in another pane also moves focus there.




=================================================================
=                    18.3 RUN CONFIGURATIONS                    =
=================================================================
 A run configuration says how this project is built and run.
 zmax keeps them per project and shows the output in the Run
 tool window.

   F5          run the active configuration
   SPC R r     the same, by name
   SPC R R     re-run whatever ran last
   SPC R c     manage configurations (Preferences: Run)
   SPC R n     jump to the next file:line in the output
   SPC R p     jump to the previous one
   SPC R l     clear the console

 :run <command> runs an arbitrary command in the same window
 and defaults to cargo run. :compile and :make are there for
 the traditional workflow, feeding the quickfix list.




=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 18 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * ! is the filter operator: !! filters the line, !G filters
   to the end of the file.
 * :pipe, :pipe-to, :insert-output and :append-output run a
   shell command against the selection.
 * :terminal opens a PTY shell; C-\ is its window leader and
   F12 detaches from it.
 * F5 runs the active run configuration; SPC R c manages them;
   SPC R n and SPC R p walk the errors in the output.











=================================================================
=                   19.1 EVALUATING A SNIPPET                   =
=================================================================
 zmax has twelve scripting interpreters compiled into the
 binary. None of them is a subprocess: there is no fork, no
 exec, and nothing to install.

   :elisp (+ x 1 2)          Emacs Lisp
   :vim echo 1 + 1           Vimscript
   :stryke say "hi"          stryke
   :ruby puts "hi"           Ruby
   :python print("hi")       Python
   :node console.log("hi")   JavaScript
   :php echo "hi";           PHP
   :tcl puts hi              Tcl
   :rlang cat("hi")          R
   :zsh print -l *           zsh
   :awk and :arb are filters; see the next lesson.

 State persists between calls, so a variable you set stays set.


=================================================================
=                  19.2 FILTERING A SELECTION                   =
=================================================================
 Two of the twelve are filters: they read the selection (or
 the whole buffer if nothing is selected) and replace it.

   :awk '{print $2}'        keep the second field of each line
   :awk 'NF'                drop the blank lines
   :arb <spec>              run an arb spec's out pipeline

 The other ten evaluate rather than filter, but bind the
 selection to stdin, so :ruby stdin.reverse and
 :php echo strtoupper($stdin); work the same way.

 Every filter lands as a single undo step, so u puts the
 buffer back exactly as it was.






=================================================================
=                    19.3 POLYGLOT PIPELINES                    =
=================================================================
 :xpipe chains those interpreters together inside this one
 process. Stages are separated by |> :

   :xpipe awk '{print $2}' |> php 'echo strtoupper($stdin);'
          |> ruby 'stdin.reverse'

 Each stage receives the previous stage's output as stdin, in
 that language's own syntax, and the whole chain is one undo
 step.

 Where :pipe spawns a shell for every selection, an N-stage
 :xpipe chain is N function calls into interpreters that are
 already linked into the binary. No editor does this; it is
 what the twelve embedded languages are for.





=================================================================
=                         19.4 THE REPL                         =
=================================================================
 SPC a r (or :repl) opens a REPL in front of all twelve
 languages. :repl ruby starts in a chosen one.

 The REPL evaluates against the live editor, so an expression
 can read and change the buffer you were editing.

 It is the fastest way to try a transformation before you
 commit to it: work out the one-liner in the REPL, then run
 the same text through :xpipe or :awk on the selection.

 The command palette (SPC SPC) lists everything the REPL can
 reach, which is a reasonable way to explore it.







=================================================================
=                      19.5 STARTUP FILES                       =
=================================================================
 If ~/.zmax/init.el or ~/.zmax/init.vim exists, zmax sources it
 at startup with the embedded elisp and Vimscript interpreters.

 That means an Emacs or vim configuration you already have can
 keep working here, rather than being rewritten into a new
 configuration language.

 :elisp and :vim evaluate the same dialects interactively, so
 a line that misbehaves in your init file can be tried on its
 own without restarting the editor.

 SPC m e b evaluates the whole buffer as elisp; SPC m e r
 evaluates just the region.






=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 19 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * Twelve interpreters are compiled in: elisp, vimscript, awk,
   zsh, stryke, ruby, php, python, node, arb, tcl and R.
 * :elisp, :vim, :ruby, :python and the rest evaluate a
   snippet against the live editor; state persists.
 * :awk and :arb filter the selection in place.
 * :xpipe chains stages with |> , all in this process.
 * SPC a r opens a REPL over all of them.
 * ~/.zmax/init.el and init.vim are sourced at startup.











=================================================================
=                     20.1 LINE OPERATIONS                      =
=================================================================
 zmax ships a large library of : commands that transform the
 selection, or the whole buffer when nothing is selected.

   :align            align the lines on = so it shares a column
   :align /:/        align on every match of a regex
   :sort             sort the selected lines
   :natural-sort     sort so file10 comes after file9
   :dedup            drop duplicate lines, keeping the first
   :reflow 72        hard-wrap the lines to 72 columns
   :trim-lines       strip trailing whitespace
   :remove-blank-lines   drop empty lines
   :rev              reverse the characters of each line
   :head 20  :tail 20   keep the first or last lines

 Each one is a single undo step.




=================================================================
=                   20.2 IDENTIFIERS AND CASE                   =
=================================================================
 Renaming a convention by hand is tedious; these do it for the
 selection.

   :to-snake        some_identifier
   :to-camel        someIdentifier
   :to-pascal       SomeIdentifier
   :to-kebab        some-identifier
   :to-constant     SOME_IDENTIFIER
   :swapcase        invert the case of every letter
   :capitalize-lines   capitalise the first word of each line
   :slugify-lines   turn each line into a URL slug

 With several cursors these run on every selection at once, so
 a whole column of names converts in one keystroke.





=================================================================
=                     20.3 STRUCTURED DATA                      =
=================================================================
 The library reshapes data without leaving the buffer.

   :json-query users.0.name   pull a value out by dot-path
   :json-flatten              rewrite JSON as path = value
   :json-sort                 sort a JSON array, or by field
   :json-to-csv               and :json-to-lines, :json-to-kv
   :csv-column 2              keep the Nth column
   :csv-to-tsv                and :tsv-to-csv
   :toml-to-json              and :json-to-toml
   :json-validate             report where the JSON breaks
   :md-table                  align a Markdown pipe table

 When a reshape needs real logic rather than a fixed rule,
 drop to :xpipe and the embedded languages.





=================================================================
=                20.4 EXTRACTING AND GENERATING                 =
=================================================================
 Extraction replaces the selection with just the parts you
 asked for, one per line.

   :extract-urls     :extract-emails     :extract-ips
   :extract-numbers  :extract-quoted     :extract-between
   :count-matches    :uniq-count         :stats
   :sum-column       :running-total      :diff-lines

 Generators insert text at every cursor:

   :uuid       a random UUID v4
   :lorem 40   forty words of placeholder text
   :date       today's date, as YYYY-MM-DD
   :seq 1 20   the numbers 1 to 20, one per line

 SPC i U 4, SPC i l p and SPC i p 2 are the leader keys for
 UUIDs, lorem paragraphs and generated passwords.


=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 20 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * :align, :sort, :dedup and :reflow reshape lines.
 * :to-snake, :to-camel, :to-pascal, :to-kebab and
   :to-constant convert identifier conventions.
 * :json-query, :json-flatten, :csv-column and friends work on
   structured data in the buffer.
 * :extract-urls and the rest of the extract family keep only
   what matched; :uuid, :lorem, :date and :seq generate text.
 * Everything here runs on the selection, or on the whole
   buffer when there is no selection, as one undo step.










=================================================================
=                    21.1 THE STATUS BUFFER                     =
=================================================================
 :magit (also :git, or SPC g s) opens a Magit-style status
 buffer: untracked, unstaged and staged sections, each of
 which can be expanded to individual hunks.

 From there you can stage and unstage by hunk, discard
 changes, commit, and reach the branch, stash and rebase
 menus, including an interactive rebase.

 SPC p v opens the same status for the project.

 The Git tool window (SPC W g) is the panel version: a list of
 changed files that stays on screen while you edit, with Enter
 opening the file under the cursor.






=================================================================
=                     21.2 HUNKS AND DIFFS                      =
=================================================================
 The gutter marks every line that differs from git HEAD.

   ]c  or  ]g     jump to the next changed hunk
   [c  or  [g     jump to the previous one
   :hunk-next     the same, by name
   :hunk-prev
   :hunk-reset    restore the hunk at the cursor from HEAD
   :diff          side-by-side diff of the buffer vs HEAD
   SPC g =        the same diff
   :diff-buffer-with-file   diff against what is on disk

 SPC b m picks among the files git says you changed, which is
 usually the right list to work through before a commit.






=================================================================
=                    21.3 BLAME AND HISTORY                     =
=================================================================
 SPC g b (or :blame) shows who last touched the current line,
 and when, in the status bar.

 SPC g t opens the file's history: every commit that touched
 it, with the file's contents at that revision.

 SPC g l l opens the current line on the remote forge in your
 browser; SPC g l L copies that link instead. The permalink
 variants (SPC g l p and SPC g l P) pin the commit, which is
 what you want when pasting a link into a review.

 SPC g H h tints the buffer by the age of the commits that
 last touched each line.






=================================================================
=                     21.4 MERGE CONFLICTS                      =
=================================================================
 After a merge that did not go cleanly, :merge (SPC g m) opens
 a three-pane resolver: ours, the result, and theirs, with the
 common ancestor available as a base pane.

 In an ordinary buffer the conflict markers are navigable:

   ]x  or  ]n     next conflict
   [x  or  [n     previous conflict
   :conflict-ours     keep our side here
   :conflict-theirs   keep their side here
   :conflict-both     keep both
   :conflict-next     and :conflict-prev

 SPC g c O and SPC g c T resolve every conflict in the file at
 once, ours or theirs.




=================================================================
=                   21.5 STAGING AND STASHING                   =
=================================================================
 The common git verbs have typed commands so they can be run
 without leaving the buffer:

   :git-stage      stage this file (git add)
   :git-unstage    unstage it again
   :stash          stash the working tree, then reload buffers
   :stash-pop      pop the most recent stash back
   :vc-root-version-diff   diff the whole tree against HEAD

 Buffers are reloaded after commands that change the working
 tree, so what is on screen matches what is on disk.

 The status buffer (:magit) remains the place to do anything
 finer-grained than a whole file.





=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 21 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * :magit (SPC g s) is the status buffer: stage, unstage,
   discard, commit, branch, stash and rebase.
 * ]c and [c walk changed hunks; :hunk-reset restores one.
 * :diff (SPC g =) diffs the buffer against HEAD.
 * SPC g b blames the current line; SPC g t browses the file's
   history; SPC g l l opens the line on the forge.
 * :merge (SPC g m) resolves conflicts in three panes; ]x and
   [x walk the markers; :conflict-ours keeps one side.
 * :git-stage, :stash and :stash-pop cover the daily verbs.










=================================================================
=                  22.1 OPENING THE WORKBENCH                   =
=================================================================
 F2 turns the IDE workbench on and off. :ide and :zen do the
 same from the command line, and zmax --ide starts in it.

 The workbench adds, around the text you were editing:

   * a project file tree on the left
   * a tree-sitter structure outline of the current file
   * problems, run and git panels along the bottom
   * an error stripe in the right margin, marking every
     diagnostic in the file at its relative position

 The layout is remembered between sessions, per project.

 Nothing about editing changes: the buffer in the middle takes
 the same keys it did before.




=================================================================
=                     22.2 FOCUSING PANELS                      =
=================================================================
 A panel only takes keys while it has focus, so each one has a
 key that focuses it:

   SPC W t    the project tree        SPC W s    the outline
   SPC W e    problems / errors       SPC W r    the run console
   SPC W g    git changes             SPC W c    CI status
   SPC W b    bookmarks               SPC W k    marks
   SPC W j    the jumplist            SPC W u    recent files
   SPC W d    the TODO list           SPC W R    registers

 Inside a panel, j and k move and Enter opens what is under
 the cursor. SPC W q hides the panel that has focus, and
 SPC W TAB returns to the panel you used last.






=================================================================
=                22.3 WINDOWS, LAYOUTS AND ZOOM                 =
=================================================================
 Splits are covered in chapter 13; the workbench adds ways to
 move around a lot of them.

   SPC 1 ... SPC 9   jump straight to window N
   SPC w s / SPC w v   split horizontally / vertically
   SPC w o           close every window but this one
   SPC l l           create or select a named layout
   SPC l n / SPC l p   cycle layouts
   SPC z z           hide the panels again (Zen mode)
   SPC z x + / -     make the text bigger or smaller
   SPC W m           maximise the bottom panel, or restore it
   SPC W f           fold the middle drawer column

 A layout is a whole window arrangement under a name, so a
 debugging layout and a writing layout are one key apart.




=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 22 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * F2 (or :ide) toggles the IDE workbench: tree, outline,
   problems, run and git panels, and an error stripe.
 * SPC W focuses a panel: t tree, s outline, e problems,
   r run, g git, c CI, and more.
 * SPC W q hides the focused panel; SPC W TAB returns to the
   last one; SPC z z hides them all.
 * SPC 1 to SPC 9 jump to a window by number.
 * SPC l l names the current window arrangement as a layout.











=================================================================
=                  23.1 BREAKPOINTS AND LAUNCH                  =
=================================================================
 zmax speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol, so any language with
 an adapter can be debugged in the editor.

   SPC d b     toggle a breakpoint on this line
   SPC d B     list every breakpoint in the project
   SPC d d     start a debug session (pick a configuration)
   F6          start debugging the current file
   SPC d q     end the session

 Conditional breakpoints and log points are edited from the
 same menu; the helix preset keeps the whole debug map on
 SPC G , where SPC G b toggles a breakpoint and SPC G l
 launches.






=================================================================
=                         23.2 STEPPING                         =
=================================================================
 Once a session is live, the stepping keys mirror every other
 debugger you have used:

   SPC d c     continue
   SPC d n     step over the next line
   SPC d i     step into the call
   SPC d o     step out of this frame
   SPC d C     run to the cursor
   SPC d p     pause a running program
   SPC d r     restart the session

 The gutter carries the state: ● is a breakpoint the adapter
 verified, ◯ one it could not bind, and the line where
 execution is paused is marked on the frame you are looking
 at. Conditional breakpoints and log points are styled
 differently again.



=================================================================
=                     23.3 INSPECTING STATE                     =
=================================================================
 SPC d v lists the variables in the selected frame.

 SPC d g t switches thread and SPC d g f switches stack frame;
 everything else follows the frame you pick.

 For gdb sessions the classic data windows are available:

   SPC d g l    locals            SPC d g r    registers
   SPC d g s    stack for thread  SPC d g d    disassembly
   SPC d g m    memory            SPC d g i    program I/O
   SPC d g e    edit a value      SPC d g w    many-windows

 Adapters are configured per language, alongside the language
 server, in languages.toml.





=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 23 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * SPC d b toggles a breakpoint; SPC d B lists them all.
 * SPC d d (or F6) starts a session; SPC d q ends it.
 * SPC d c continues, n steps over, i steps in, o steps out,
   and C runs to the cursor.
 * SPC d v shows variables; SPC d g t and SPC d g f change
   thread and frame.
 * The gdb data windows are on SPC d g l, r, s, d, m and i.
 * In the helix preset the whole map lives under SPC G .











=================================================================
=                         24.1 SNIPPETS                         =
=================================================================
 :snippets opens the snippet library: create, edit and delete
 reusable pieces of text, stored in snippets.toml and scoped
 by language.

 To use one, type its trigger word in insert mode and press
 Tab. The body is inserted with its tab stops live, so Tab
 walks from ${1:...} to ${2:...} and finally to $0.

 Tab is doing three jobs in insert mode, in order: it accepts
 an AI ghost-text suggestion if one is showing, then expands a
 snippet or an Emmet abbreviation, and otherwise inserts a
 tab. You never have to decide which.

 Emmet abbreviations work in HTML-ish buffers: type
 ul>li*3 and press Tab.




=================================================================
=                      24.2 THE HEX EDITOR                      =
=================================================================
 :hex opens an xxd-style view of a file's raw bytes: offset,
 hex columns, and the printable text on the right.

 Binary files open here instead of being refused, so a stray
 zmax on a compiled object shows you something useful.

 :hex <path> views a file other than the current buffer, and
 SPC f h opens the picker straight into the hex view.

 Tab switches between the hex and the text column, editing
 either one, and C-s writes the bytes back to the file.

 :hexdump is the transform-library cousin: it rewrites the
 selection as a hex dump inside the buffer, which is handy for
 pasting bytes into a bug report.




=================================================================
=                         24.3 ORG MODE                         =
=================================================================
 Org files get outline editing, TODO cycling and an agenda.

   SPC m TAB     fold or unfold the subtree
   SPC m t       cycle the TODO keyword
   SPC m p       cycle the priority cookie
   SPC m H       promote the heading
   SPC m l       demote it
   SPC m j / k   next / previous heading
   SPC m a / A   fold or unfold everything
   SPC a o c     capture a new note
   SPC a o a     open the agenda (also :org-agenda)

 The agenda collects the TODO and DONE items from the open
 .org buffers and the .org files under the working directory,
 grouped by scheduled and deadline date.




=================================================================
=                     24.4 THE HELP BROWSER                     =
=================================================================
 :help (SPC h h) opens a searchable browser over every
 command, every key and every topic zmax knows about.

   SPC h k     describe a key
   SPC h c     describe a command
   SPC h ?     list the bindings
   SPC h f     browse the FAQ
   SPC h n     browse the release notes
   SPC h l     search the capability areas
   SPC h m     search the man pages
   SPC h i     search the info manuals
   SPC h d f   describe the function at the cursor, via LSP

 Outside the editor, zmax --health reports the config and
 language files, the runtime directories, and per language
 the servers, debug adapter, formatter and queries.
 :version reports the build you are running.


=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 24 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * :snippets manages reusable snippets; type a trigger and
   press Tab to expand it with live tab stops.
 * Tab in insert mode accepts ghost text, then expands a
   snippet or Emmet abbreviation, then inserts a tab.
 * :hex is a byte-level view; binary files open in it.
 * Org buffers fold with SPC m TAB, cycle TODO with SPC m t,
   and collect into an agenda with SPC a o a.
 * :help (SPC h h) searches commands, keys and topics; the
   SPC h family describes keys, commands and symbols.










=================================================================
=                      25.1 NATIVE PLUGINS                      =
=================================================================
 Beyond the twelve embedded languages, zmax loads native
 plugins: ordinary compiled Rust cdylibs, over a frozen C ABI,
 with no editor rebuild.

   :zmax-native load <path>     load a built plugin
   :zmax-native list            what is loaded now
   :zmax-native unload <name>   drop it again

 A plugin registers its own : commands and can read and edit
 the buffer, run command lines and post status messages
 through the host API.

 The SDK crate is zmax-native, and the repository ships
 buildable examples: hello, insert-date, buffer-stats,
 trim-trailing and banner.




=================================================================
=                   25.2 THE PACKAGE MANAGER                    =
=================================================================
 The same command is a package manager for those plugins.

   :zmax-native add owner/repo        clone, build, install
   :zmax-native add owner/repo@v1.2   pin a tag or commit
   :zmax-native add path:./my-plugin  a local checkout
   :zmax-native get owner/repo        install if absent, else
                                      load from the store
   :zmax-native sync                  load everything installed
   :zmax-native registry              list what is installed
   :zmax-native update / remove / info
   :zmax-native gc --dry-run          what a clean-up removes

 Installs land in a content-addressed store under ~/.zmax/pkg,
 SHA-256 pinned, and load by mmap rather than by copying.





=================================================================
=                 25.3 CONFIGURATION AND THEMES                 =
=================================================================
 zmax is meant to be useful with no configuration at all, but
 everything is adjustable.

   :config-open      your config.toml
   :config-open-workspace   the per-project one
   :theme <name>     switch theme now
   SPC T c           pick a theme from a list
   :theme-next       and :theme-prev, to browse them
   :keymap <preset>  spacemacs, vim, helix, kakoune, emacs,
                     cua, micro or nano
   SPC ,             the Preferences window
   SPC S             its Settings tab

 Config changes take effect without a restart.





=================================================================
=                       CHAPTER 25 RECAP                        =
=================================================================
 * :zmax-native load, list and unload manage compiled plugins;
   the SDK crate is zmax-native.
 * :zmax-native add, get, sync, update, remove and gc are the
   package manager over a content-addressed store.
 * :config-open edits config.toml; :theme and SPC T c change
   the theme; :keymap changes the whole keymap preset.
 * SPC , opens Preferences.












